d per-call diagnostics\". The output schema for normalized_gain is number, but no specific rounding. Likely round to some decimal places? In the example fixtures, the numbers have many decimals. Could be round to 8 decimal places? But the schema doesn't specify. We could round to a reasonable number like 8 or 10, but to be safe, we can use round(normalized_gain, 10) or just keep as float? The JSON output will serialize float. Usually it's fine. However, the instruction \"rounded per-call diagnostics\" suggests we should round. I'll round to 8 decimal places as a common choice, but I'll check: The schema says \"normalized_gain\" type number, no constraint on precision. We can just compute and leave as float; JSON serialization will produce full precision. The instruction \"rounded\" might mean we should round to some consistent precision to avoid floating point artifacts. I'll round to 10 decimal places.\n\nAlso note: \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: normalized_weights, stronger_counterfactual, inclusive_threshold, stable_order.\" Not sure what these refer to. Possibly from the task description above? Actually the task description says \"normalized_weights, stronger_counterfactual, inclusive_threshold, stable_order.\" But the input only has threshold and calls. \"normalized_weights\" might mean we divide by prediction_tokens. \"stronger_counterfactual\" means we subtract loss_with_call from loss_without_call. \"inclusive_threshold\" means normalized_gain >= threshold (inclusive). \"stable_order\" means keep order of calls as given.\n\nSo we need to iterate over calls, compute normalized_gain = (loss_without - loss_with) / prediction_tokens, then accepted if parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold. Then build diagnostics list with accepted, call_id, normalized_gain. Also accepted_call_ids list of accepted ones.\n\nReturn dict with keys accepted_call_ids and diagnostics.\n\nWe must not mutate case.\n\nWe need to out