need escaping. However, we have newlines that must be escaped as \\n. Also we have backslashes? Not in the code. But we have double quotes? No. So we can just replace newlines with \\n.\n\nBut careful: The code contains single quotes that are fine. We should also escape any backslashes if present. There are none.\n\nWe also need to ensure that the code's indentation is preserved. Using \\n with spaces.\n\nLet's write the implementation string properly.\n\nI'll produce the output as:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    accepted_call_ids = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    for call in calls:\\n        gain = call['loss_without_call'] - call['loss_with_call']\\n        normalized_gain = gain / call['prediction_tokens']\\n        normalized_gain = round(normalized_gain, 10)\\n        accepted = call['parseable'] and normalized_gain >= threshold\\n        diagnostics.append({\\n            'accepted': accepted,\\n            'call_id': call['call_id'],\\n            'normalized_gain': normalized_gain\\n        })\\n        if accepted:\\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call['call_id'])\\n    return {\\n        'accepted_call_ids': accepted_call_ids,\\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\\n    }\"}\n\nBut note: The JSON string must be a single line? No, it can have newlines in the string value, but to be safe, we should escape them as \\n. Actually, JSON allows literal newlines in a string? According to JSON spec, newlines must be escaped as \\n. So we must replace newlines with \\n. The above I wrote with actual newlines, but that would be invalid JSON. So we need to convert to a single line with \\n.\n\nLet's do that manually. Also we need to escape any double quotes inside the string. There are no double quotes.\n\nSo the implementation string becomes:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    accepted_call_ids = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n 